1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,993
Picture a modern wedding.

2
00:00:02,013 --> 00:00:03,443
The dress. The rings.

3
00:00:03,463 --> 00:00:07,319
The cake. The vows, and a piece
of paper that makes it official.

4
00:00:07,339 --> 00:00:12,034
In the United States, the average wedding
now costs about thirty four thousand

5
00:00:12,054 --> 00:00:16,609
dollars. That's for around a hundred
and seventeen guests, at nearly three

6
00:00:16,629 --> 00:00:18,414
hundred dollars each.

7
00:00:18,434 --> 00:00:21,315
And yet people keep doing
it, all over the world.

8
00:00:21,335 --> 00:00:26,427
Anthropologists have found some kind of
marriage in every known human culture.

9
00:00:26,447 --> 00:00:28,194
So where did it begin?

10
00:00:28,214 --> 00:00:30,736
Now take time back fifty thousand years.

11
00:00:30,756 --> 00:00:34,070
A young woman and a young
man sit together by the fire.

12
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:35,295
No rings. No priest.

13
00:00:35,315 --> 00:00:37,284
No paper. No wedding at all.

14
00:00:37,304 --> 00:00:39,534
So how did two people become a couple?

15
00:00:39,554 --> 00:00:41,476
And who got to decide?

16
00:00:41,496 --> 00:00:44,501
Now your brain is
probably saying, Cavemen?

17
00:00:44,521 --> 00:00:49,301
That's easy. A man hit a woman over the
head with a club, and dragged her home by

18
00:00:49,321 --> 00:00:51,871
the hair. At least, that's what we assume.

19
00:00:51,891 --> 00:00:54,450
But that picture doesn't
come from archaeology.

20
00:00:54,470 --> 00:00:59,528
It grew out of Victorian theories about
marriage by capture, dreamed up by a

21
00:00:59,548 --> 00:01:04,128
Scottish lawyer in the eighteen sixties,
who never studied a hunter-gatherer

22
00:01:04,148 --> 00:01:09,383
society. Then novels and films turned
it into a joke, like Buster Keaton 's

23
00:01:09,403 --> 00:01:12,708
comedy The Three Ages, in
nineteen twenty three.

24
00:01:12,728 --> 00:01:15,130
There's no evidence for it at all.

25
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:17,930
The real story is far more interesting.

26
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:22,517
It involves love that shows up in
almost every culture on Earth.

27
00:01:22,537 --> 00:01:26,055
Ancient family trees, rebuilt from DNA.

28
00:01:26,075 --> 00:01:31,104
And something so important, it may have
helped create human society itself.

29
00:01:31,124 --> 00:01:35,094
To understand human marriage,
start with how our cousins pair up.

30
00:01:35,114 --> 00:01:37,356
Gibbons usually live as pairs.

31
00:01:37,376 --> 00:01:41,282
A male and a female, singing
duets together in the trees.

32
00:01:41,302 --> 00:01:44,509
Though even gibbons aren't
always perfectly faithful.

33
00:01:44,529 --> 00:01:50,009
Gorillas usually live in groups with
one big adult male and several females,

34
00:01:50,029 --> 00:01:52,630
though some groups have
more than one male.

35
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:58,148
Chimpanzees and bonobos live in bigger
mixed groups, with no lasting couples.

36
00:01:58,168 --> 00:01:59,873
And there's a clue in their bodies.

37
00:01:59,893 --> 00:02:04,836
In species where males compete hard for
mates, males tend to be much bigger than

38
00:02:04,856 --> 00:02:10,693
females. A male gorilla can weigh about
twice as much as a female, or more.

39
00:02:10,713 --> 00:02:13,618
Male chimps are about
thirty percent heavier.

40
00:02:13,638 --> 00:02:18,580
And men are only about fifteen percent
heavier than women, on average.

41
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,768
That puts us somewhere in between.

42
00:02:20,788 --> 00:02:21,905
Not quite gibbons.

43
00:02:21,925 --> 00:02:23,559
Not quite gorillas.

44
00:02:23,579 --> 00:02:24,984
Humans are flexible.

45
00:02:25,004 --> 00:02:28,211
But one thing keeps showing
up, again and again.

46
00:02:28,231 --> 00:02:29,305
Pairs.

47
00:02:29,325 --> 00:02:30,943
Why do humans pair up?

48
00:02:30,963 --> 00:02:33,485
Part of the answer seems
to be in the brain.

49
00:02:33,505 --> 00:02:37,847
Scientists have studied two kinds
of small rodents called voles.

50
00:02:37,867 --> 00:02:41,385
Prairie voles often form
long-lasting pairs.

51
00:02:41,405 --> 00:02:44,339
Their cousins, meadow voles, don't.

52
00:02:44,359 --> 00:02:49,045
In two thousand four, researchers added
extra receptors for a hormone called

53
00:02:49,065 --> 00:02:54,382
vasopressin to one reward area in
the brains of male meadow voles.

54
00:02:54,402 --> 00:02:58,770
Those males started preferring to
stay close to one familiar partner.

55
00:02:58,790 --> 00:03:02,945
They spent much more time huddled side
by side with her, rather than with a

56
00:03:02,965 --> 00:03:07,364
stranger. Humans are much
more complicated than voles.

57
00:03:07,384 --> 00:03:11,327
But it shows the urge to bond
can be built into the brain.

58
00:03:11,347 --> 00:03:12,651
And what about love?

59
00:03:12,671 --> 00:03:16,230
Some people think romantic
love is a modern invention.

60
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:21,172
In nineteen ninety two, two anthropologists
searched the records of a hundred and

61
00:03:21,192 --> 00:03:23,785
sixty six cultures around the world.

62
00:03:23,805 --> 00:03:28,754
They found evidence of romantic love
in a hundred and forty seven of them.

63
00:03:28,774 --> 00:03:32,251
For the rest, they suspected
the records just had gaps.

64
00:03:32,271 --> 00:03:34,063
Love songs. Love poems.

65
00:03:34,083 --> 00:03:36,054
Couples who ran away together.

66
00:03:36,074 --> 00:03:40,883
In the British Museum, there's a small
carving from near Bethlehem, about eleven

67
00:03:40,903 --> 00:03:42,409
thousand years old.

68
00:03:42,429 --> 00:03:47,379
It's made from a single pebble, about ten
centimetres tall, and shows two people

69
00:03:47,399 --> 00:03:49,021
in a close embrace.

70
00:03:49,041 --> 00:03:51,321
It looks different from every angle.

71
00:03:51,341 --> 00:03:55,123
And it's often called the
oldest known image of a couple.

72
00:03:55,143 --> 00:03:57,700
But love alone doesn't explain marriage.

73
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,300
Something else matters too.

74
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:03,369
Human babies. A human baby
is helpless for years.

75
00:04:03,389 --> 00:04:07,181
And raising one takes a huge
amount of food and work.

76
00:04:07,201 --> 00:04:10,151
A mother with a baby can't
easily do it all alone.

77
00:04:10,171 --> 00:04:11,188
Grandmothers help.

78
00:04:11,208 --> 00:04:12,676
Older children help.

79
00:04:12,696 --> 00:04:16,327
And in many societies,
fathers help a lot too.

80
00:04:16,347 --> 00:04:21,352
Among the Aka of Central Africa, fathers
are within arm's reach of their babies

81
00:04:21,372 --> 00:04:23,315
for about half of each day.

82
00:04:23,335 --> 00:04:26,520
Holding them, playing with
them, comforting them.

83
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:31,245
Some researchers think care like this
helped favour lasting pair bonds.

84
00:04:31,265 --> 00:04:36,893
Two adults, sharing the work of raising
children who take so long to grow up.

85
00:04:36,913 --> 00:04:39,963
So what did marriage look
like among hunter-gatherers?

86
00:04:39,983 --> 00:04:44,950
Among the Hadza of Tanzania, marriages
usually aren't arranged, and there's no

87
00:04:44,970 --> 00:04:49,908
ceremony at all. A couple counts as
married once they start living together.

88
00:04:49,928 --> 00:04:50,996
That's it. No party.

89
00:04:51,016 --> 00:04:53,764
No paperwork. And if it doesn't work out?

90
00:04:53,784 --> 00:04:56,039
They can separate, just as simply.

91
00:04:56,059 --> 00:05:01,352
Divorce is fairly common, especially in
the first years, and most Hadza adults

92
00:05:01,372 --> 00:05:03,064
marry more than once.

93
00:05:03,084 --> 00:05:07,395
And only about four percent of
marriages have more than one wife.

94
00:05:07,415 --> 00:05:12,045
When the anthropologist Frank Marlowe
asked Hadza people what they wanted in a

95
00:05:12,065 --> 00:05:15,951
husband or wife, the
answers were very practical.

96
00:05:15,971 --> 00:05:19,888
Both men and women said good
character mattered most.

97
00:05:19,908 --> 00:05:24,876
Women also cared about intelligence,
and about a man being a good forager.

98
00:05:24,896 --> 00:05:28,196
Men also cared about a
wife who worked hard.

99
00:05:28,216 --> 00:05:30,233
Body size hardly mattered.

100
00:05:30,253 --> 00:05:34,648
Only one woman out of forty six
said she wanted someone big.

101
00:05:34,668 --> 00:05:37,786
In the Kalahari, the
Ju'hoansi had another custom.

102
00:05:37,806 --> 00:05:39,727
It's called bride service.

103
00:05:39,747 --> 00:05:44,352
After the wedding, the young husband moved
in with his wife's family, and hunted

104
00:05:44,372 --> 00:05:49,066
for them, often for several
years, sometimes as long as ten.

105
00:05:49,086 --> 00:05:52,447
After that, the couple could
choose which family to live with.

106
00:05:52,467 --> 00:05:55,216
First marriages were
often arranged by parents.

107
00:05:55,236 --> 00:06:00,284
But in the famous book Nisa, a Ju'hoansi
woman remembers running away from early

108
00:06:00,304 --> 00:06:02,209
husbands she didn't want.

109
00:06:02,229 --> 00:06:06,134
Divorce was possible, and was
often started by the wife.

110
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:09,772
Close to half of first
marriages ended that way.

111
00:06:09,792 --> 00:06:13,985
And in many societies, marriage
brought a surprising new rule.

112
00:06:14,005 --> 00:06:16,553
Keep your distance from
your mother-in-law.

113
00:06:16,573 --> 00:06:21,616
Speakers of the Dyirbal language in
Queensland, Australia, traditionally avoided

114
00:06:21,636 --> 00:06:25,928
talking directly to certain in-laws,
or looking them in the eye.

115
00:06:25,948 --> 00:06:30,178
And when they were nearby, people switched
to a special mother-in-law language,

116
00:06:30,198 --> 00:06:34,001
with the same grammar, but a
whole different set of words.

117
00:06:34,021 --> 00:06:38,789
So be honest. Would a mother-in-law language
make your family dinners easier, or

118
00:06:38,809 --> 00:06:41,349
harder? Tell me in the comments.

119
00:06:41,369 --> 00:06:44,274
For marriages long ago,
we can't ask anyone.

120
00:06:44,294 --> 00:06:46,542
But we can read their DNA.

121
00:06:46,562 --> 00:06:51,642
Remember the two boys buried at Sungir,
in Russia, about thirty four thousand

122
00:06:51,662 --> 00:06:56,971
years ago? When scientists read the DNA
of four people buried there, none were

123
00:06:56,991 --> 00:06:59,991
more closely related than second cousins.

124
00:07:00,011 --> 00:07:04,666
Even though people lived in small bands,
they seem to have found partners from a

125
00:07:04,686 --> 00:07:06,503
much wider network.

126
00:07:06,523 --> 00:07:08,079
Neanderthals were different.

127
00:07:08,099 --> 00:07:13,912
In a cave in Siberia, scientists read
the DNA of a group of Neanderthals, about

128
00:07:13,932 --> 00:07:16,224
fifty four thousand years old.

129
00:07:16,244 --> 00:07:18,562
Thirteen individuals in all.

130
00:07:18,582 --> 00:07:21,705
And among them, a father
and his teenage daughter.

131
00:07:21,725 --> 00:07:26,580
They seem to have lived in tiny
communities of about ten to twenty people.

132
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:32,404
And the DNA suggests the women were the
ones who more often moved between groups.

133
00:07:32,424 --> 00:07:36,575
In a nearby cave, there was
a girl scientists call Denny.

134
00:07:36,595 --> 00:07:38,537
Her mother was a Neanderthal.

135
00:07:38,557 --> 00:07:42,937
Her father was a Denisovan, a
different kind of human altogether.

136
00:07:42,957 --> 00:07:48,212
About ninety thousand years ago, two
different human groups met, and had a child.

137
00:07:48,232 --> 00:07:50,437
She lived to at least thirteen.

138
00:07:50,457 --> 00:07:55,121
And her Denisovan father had some
Neanderthal ancestors of his own.

139
00:07:55,141 --> 00:07:58,490
All of this came from one
small fragment of her bone.

140
00:07:58,510 --> 00:08:01,833
Then the farmers came, and
their graves tell us even more.

141
00:08:01,853 --> 00:08:06,750
At Gurgy, in France, about six thousand
seven hundred years ago, people were

142
00:08:06,770 --> 00:08:09,505
buried together as one big family.

143
00:08:09,525 --> 00:08:15,255
Scientists read the DNA of ninety four
people, and rebuilt a family tree of sixty

144
00:08:15,275 --> 00:08:18,692
four relatives. Seven generations long.

145
00:08:18,712 --> 00:08:20,717
The men were mostly born there.

146
00:08:20,737 --> 00:08:23,482
Most of the women came
from somewhere else.

147
00:08:23,502 --> 00:08:26,307
And the grown-up daughters
are almost missing.

148
00:08:26,327 --> 00:08:30,376
They seem to have moved away, to
marry into other communities.

149
00:08:30,396 --> 00:08:35,289
There were no half-brothers or half-sisters
at all, which suggests couples mostly

150
00:08:35,309 --> 00:08:41,271
stayed together. At Hazleton North, in
England, a stone tomb about five thousand

151
00:08:41,291 --> 00:08:45,705
seven hundred years old held
five generations of one family.

152
00:08:45,725 --> 00:08:50,846
Most of them descended from one man, and
four women with whom he had children.

153
00:08:50,866 --> 00:08:55,433
And in the Lech valley in southern Germany,
about four thousand years ago, most

154
00:08:55,453 --> 00:09:00,058
of the women had grown up far away,
perhaps hundreds of kilometres.

155
00:09:00,078 --> 00:09:03,097
They were often buried
with rich jewellery.

156
00:09:03,117 --> 00:09:08,622
These households also included poorer,
unrelated people, a sign that inequality

157
00:09:08,642 --> 00:09:13,683
had arrived too. They carried new
ideas, and new connections, with them.

158
00:09:13,703 --> 00:09:16,483
So what's the natural
way for humans to marry?

159
00:09:16,503 --> 00:09:22,130
There isn't one. In one famous survey
of more than twelve hundred societies,

160
00:09:22,150 --> 00:09:26,509
about eighty five percent allowed
a man to have more than one wife.

161
00:09:26,529 --> 00:09:30,253
Yet even in those societies,
most men had just one.

162
00:09:30,273 --> 00:09:33,753
About fifteen percent
allowed only one spouse.

163
00:09:33,773 --> 00:09:37,566
And just four allowed a woman
to have more than one husband.

164
00:09:37,586 --> 00:09:42,853
Though later researchers found dozens more
societies where it sometimes happened.

165
00:09:42,873 --> 00:09:47,550
In the mountains of northwest Nepal,
among the Nyinba, a group of brothers

166
00:09:47,570 --> 00:09:52,886
traditionally married one wife together,
keeping the family farm in one piece.

167
00:09:52,906 --> 00:09:57,448
And among the Mosuo of southwest China,
couples traditionally keep separate

168
00:09:57,468 --> 00:10:02,605
homes. In what's called a walking marriage,
the man visits at night, and children

169
00:10:02,625 --> 00:10:07,117
grow up in their mother's household,
often with their uncles helping to raise

170
00:10:07,137 --> 00:10:12,345
them. Many fathers are still
involved, and the custom is changing.

171
00:10:12,365 --> 00:10:14,258
Marriage usually came with gifts.

172
00:10:14,278 --> 00:10:19,089
In about two thirds of societies, the
groom's family gave gifts or wealth to the

173
00:10:19,109 --> 00:10:22,226
bride's family. That's called bridewealth.

174
00:10:22,246 --> 00:10:27,524
A dowry, where wealth goes with the bride,
appears in fewer than four percent.

175
00:10:27,544 --> 00:10:29,313
Then marriage got written down.

176
00:10:29,333 --> 00:10:33,775
Some of the oldest marriage agreements
we know come from the ancient Near East,

177
00:10:33,795 --> 00:10:35,288
pressed into clay.

178
00:10:35,308 --> 00:10:40,313
One tablet, written about four thousand
years ago by Assyrian merchants in what

179
00:10:40,333 --> 00:10:45,451
is now Turkey, even said what would happen
if the couple had no child within two

180
00:10:45,471 --> 00:10:51,124
years. Hammurabi's laws, about three
thousand seven hundred and fifty years old,

181
00:10:51,144 --> 00:10:54,908
set rules for marriage
gifts, dowries and divorce.

182
00:10:54,928 --> 00:10:59,358
In ancient Egypt, marriage seems to
have been a private arrangement, not a

183
00:10:59,378 --> 00:11:00,994
religious ceremony.

184
00:11:01,014 --> 00:11:04,542
The words for it meant
something like founding a house.

185
00:11:04,562 --> 00:11:08,667
Women kept the property they brought into
a marriage, and either partner could

186
00:11:08,687 --> 00:11:14,317
start a divorce. After a divorce, a woman
could take back her own property, plus

187
00:11:14,337 --> 00:11:17,454
a share of what the couple
had built up together.

188
00:11:17,474 --> 00:11:20,504
The Romans gave us something
many people still use.

189
00:11:20,524 --> 00:11:26,008
Betrothal rings. A Roman writer, Aulus
Gellius, reported an old claim that a

190
00:11:26,028 --> 00:11:30,296
delicate nerve ran from the fourth
finger of the left hand, straight to the

191
00:11:30,316 --> 00:11:32,183
heart. It isn't true.

192
00:11:32,203 --> 00:11:35,928
But many people still wear a
wedding ring on that finger.

193
00:11:35,948 --> 00:11:39,265
And sometimes the past leaves
us something very tender.

194
00:11:39,285 --> 00:11:44,460
In two thousand seven, near Mantua in
Italy, archaeologists found two young

195
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:49,422
people buried about six thousand years
ago, facing each other, with their arms

196
00:11:49,442 --> 00:11:50,691
around each other.

197
00:11:50,711 --> 00:11:54,004
They were only about eighteen
to twenty years old.

198
00:11:54,024 --> 00:11:58,691
They're known as the Lovers of Valdaro,
and you can still see them in a museum in

199
00:11:58,711 --> 00:12:04,402
Mantua. In Modena, two people buried
hand in hand about fifteen hundred years

200
00:12:04,422 --> 00:12:06,827
ago turned out to both be men.

201
00:12:06,847 --> 00:12:09,502
Nobody knows what their relationship was.

202
00:12:09,522 --> 00:12:12,396
But someone chose to bury them together.

203
00:12:12,416 --> 00:12:16,089
For most of history, though,
marriage wasn't mainly about love.

204
00:12:16,109 --> 00:12:21,201
The historian Stephanie Coontz found that
marriage was usually about property,

205
00:12:21,221 --> 00:12:23,239
alliances and work.

206
00:12:23,259 --> 00:12:28,014
She said she was surprised how unanimous
people were, until the late seventeen

207
00:12:28,034 --> 00:12:31,573
hundreds, that love was
not a good reason to marry.

208
00:12:31,593 --> 00:12:34,397
Land. Money. Connections between families.

209
00:12:34,417 --> 00:12:39,823
The idea that you should marry mainly
for love only became the ideal in Western

210
00:12:39,843 --> 00:12:43,771
Europe and North America from
the late seventeen hundreds.

211
00:12:43,791 --> 00:12:46,675
Many wedding traditions
are newer than you'd think.

212
00:12:46,695 --> 00:12:52,100
When Queen Victoria married in eighteen
forty, she wore white silk and lace, and

213
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:55,150
helped make white wedding
dresses fashionable.

214
00:12:55,170 --> 00:12:59,811
She chose English lace on purpose,
to help lace makers in Devon.

215
00:12:59,831 --> 00:13:04,936
And the diamond engagement ring took off
after a copywriter came up with a famous

216
00:13:04,956 --> 00:13:07,436
slogan in nineteen forty seven.

217
00:13:07,456 --> 00:13:09,486
A diamond is forever.

218
00:13:09,506 --> 00:13:14,061
In nineteen ninety nine, an advertising
magazine named it the slogan of the

219
00:13:14,081 --> 00:13:15,265
century.

220
00:13:15,285 --> 00:13:16,990
So what was marriage really for?

221
00:13:17,010 --> 00:13:19,172
Here's one powerful idea.

222
00:13:19,192 --> 00:13:20,596
Think back to the chimps.

223
00:13:20,616 --> 00:13:24,328
A chimp has no lasting
partner, so it has no in-laws.

224
00:13:24,348 --> 00:13:27,836
But when humans formed lasting
pairs, something new appeared.

225
00:13:27,856 --> 00:13:29,111
Your partner's parents.

226
00:13:29,131 --> 00:13:30,611
Their brothers and sisters.

227
00:13:30,631 --> 00:13:34,261
Their whole band. Suddenly,
they were family too.

228
00:13:34,281 --> 00:13:39,411
The primatologist Bernard Chapais argues
that this was a turning point, and that

229
00:13:39,431 --> 00:13:42,782
recognising in-laws is uniquely human.

230
00:13:42,802 --> 00:13:45,532
Marriages linked one band to another.

231
00:13:45,552 --> 00:13:50,245
And groups linked by marriage became
relatives, which made peaceful ties between

232
00:13:50,265 --> 00:13:55,217
them possible. In the Kalahari, people
also kept gift-exchange partners in

233
00:13:55,237 --> 00:14:00,437
faraway camps. In the nineteen seventies,
the average person had about sixteen of

234
00:14:00,457 --> 00:14:04,987
them, some two hundred kilometres away,
and spent around three months a year

235
00:14:05,007 --> 00:14:10,168
visiting. Marriages and friendships like
these built bridges between groups.

236
00:14:10,188 --> 00:14:13,618
Seen this way, marriage
wasn't just about two people.

237
00:14:13,638 --> 00:14:18,054
It was how separate bands
became one big human network.

238
00:14:18,074 --> 00:14:21,103
Today, weddings are bigger
and fancier than ever.

239
00:14:21,123 --> 00:14:23,717
Rings, dresses, flowers, cakes.

240
00:14:23,737 --> 00:14:26,105
And yet, the oldest parts are still there.

241
00:14:26,125 --> 00:14:28,030
Two families, meeting each other.

242
00:14:28,050 --> 00:14:30,543
A feast. Gifts. Promises.

243
00:14:30,563 --> 00:14:33,767
And the hope that two people
will look after each other.

244
00:14:33,787 --> 00:14:37,030
Before we go, which custom
surprised you most?

245
00:14:37,050 --> 00:14:40,856
Bride service, the mother-in-law
language, or walking marriage?

246
00:14:40,876 --> 00:14:42,864
Let me know in the comments.

247
00:14:42,884 --> 00:14:47,106
Fifty thousand years ago, a young
couple sat together by the fire.

248
00:14:47,126 --> 00:14:48,319
They had no rings.

249
00:14:48,339 --> 00:14:50,281
No paper. No cake.

250
00:14:50,301 --> 00:14:54,319
But when they got together, two families
became one, and two bands became

251
00:14:54,339 --> 00:14:56,866
friends. You marry one person.

252
00:14:56,886 --> 00:14:59,311
They married into a whole world.
